---
import Layout from "@/layouts/Layout.astro";
import Header from "@/components/Header.astro";
import Main from "@/components/Main.astro";
import Footer from "@/components/Footer.astro";
import ProjectCard from "@/components/ProjectCard.astro";
import { PROJECTS } from "@/data/projects";
import config from "@/config";

const prose = "app-prose mt-6 [&_h3]:text-lg [&_li]:my-1";
---

<Layout title={`Open source projects | ${config.site.title}`}>
  <Header />

  <Main
    pageTitle="Open source projects"
    pageDesc="Things I build and maintain myself."
  >
    <section id="ai-exporter" class="card p-5 sm:p-8">
      <ProjectCard project={PROJECTS.aiExporter} headingLevel="h2" />

      <div class={prose}>
        <h3>What it does</h3>
        <ul>
          <li>
            Works on fifteen AI chat sites: ChatGPT, Claude, Gemini, DeepSeek,
            Grok, Perplexity, Microsoft Copilot, Mistral Le Chat, Meta AI, Kimi,
            Doubao, Qwen, Qianwen, Tencent Yuanbao and Z.ai.
          </li>
          <li>
            Exports to nine formats: PDF, Word, a self-contained web page, one
            long image, Markdown, plain text, JSON, CSV and Excel.
          </li>
          <li>
            Saves many chats at once into a ZIP, and can back up new and changed
            chats every hour, day or week to your Downloads folder or a private
            GitHub repository.
          </li>
          <li>
            Saves straight to GitHub or Notion, opens a chat in Google Docs,
            continues it in another AI, and gives a ready citation in APA, MLA
            and Chicago style.
          </li>
          <li>Interface in thirteen languages.</li>
        </ul>

        <h3>How it's built</h3>
        <ul>
          <li>
            It reads each conversation through the same API the chat site's own
            web app uses, instead of scrolling the page and scraping whatever is
            rendered. That's why long chats come out complete and in the right
            order, and why regenerated replies and edited messages follow the
            branch you're actually looking at. Mistral Le Chat and Meta AI have
            no such API, so on those two it reads the page.
          </li>
          <li>
            PDFs embed fonts for Latin, Cyrillic, Greek, Hebrew, Arabic,
            Chinese, Japanese, Korean, the Indian scripts and more. Complex
            scripts are shaped with HarfBuzz, right-to-left text stays
            right-to-left, and formulas are typeset. In Word they become real,
            editable equations.
          </li>
          <li>
            Everything runs locally. There is no AI Exporter server, no account
            and no analytics. The extension only talks to the chat site you're
            exporting from, and to GitHub or Notion if you use those features.
          </li>
          <li>
            Written in TypeScript. The source is on GitHub and free for personal
            and other noncommercial use.
          </li>
        </ul>

        <h3>Installing it</h3>
        <ul>
          <li>
            <strong>Chrome and Edge:</strong> from the{" "}
            <a
              href="https://chromewebstore.google.com/detail/objkcakdcilfaphifjfcgfamlnnbinjc"
              >Chrome Web Store</a
            >. Edge installs Chrome extensions after you allow extensions from
            other stores.
          </li>
          <li>
            <strong>Firefox 140 or newer:</strong> build it from{" "}
            <a
              href="https://github.com/GrantTotinov/AIExporter#from-source-chrome-edge-or-firefox"
              >the source</a
            >.
          </li>
        </ul>
      </div>
    </section>

    <section id="plainsight" class="card mt-6 p-5 sm:p-8">
      <ProjectCard project={PROJECTS.plainSight} headingLevel="h2" />

      <div class={prose}>
        <h3>What it does</h3>
        <ul>
          <li>
            Hides the AI Overview and the AI Mode buttons and tabs on Google
            Search, on the results page and on the Google homepage. The rest of
            the page stays as it is.
          </li>
          <li>
            Web only mode opens every search in Google's own Web filter, which
            shows links only. Because the filter runs on Google's side, it keeps
            working when Google changes its page layout.
          </li>
          <li>
            Optional: hide "People also ask", whose answers Google now writes
            with AI, and send AI Mode back to regular results.
          </li>
          <li>
            Want the AI answer once? Show it on that page with one click, or
            pause a tab with Alt+Shift+H. Works on every Google domain, and the
            interface is in 19 languages.
          </li>
        </ul>

        <h3>How it's built</h3>
        <ul>
          <li>
            Web only uses <code>declarativeNetRequest</code> to add the Web filter
            (<code>udm=14</code>) to plain searches before the page loads. A
            search where you already picked a tab, such as Images or News, is
            left alone, and your default search engine doesn't change.
          </li>
          <li>
            Hide AI injects CSS before the page renders, so nothing flashes, and
            finds AI blocks in five independent ways: known selectors, the
            heading text in 19 languages, the AI Mode labels, accessibility
            labels and AI Mode links. Every match goes through the same safety
            check, so a hidden block never contains a regular search result.
          </li>
          <li>
            Fixes for Google page changes arrive once a day as a data-only
            selector file from GitHub, without waiting for a store review. The
            request carries no cookies or identifiers, and you can turn it off.
          </li>
          <li>
            No analytics, no tracking and no remote code. Built with WXT and
            Preact, licensed under MIT.
          </li>
          <li>
            The AI Mode button in Chrome's address bar and new tab page is part
            of the browser, not of the page, so no extension can remove it.
          </li>
        </ul>

        <h3>Installing it</h3>
        <ul>
          <li>
            The Chrome Web Store, Edge Add-ons and Firefox Add-ons listings are
            on their way.
          </li>
          <li>
            Until then, builds for Chrome, Edge and Firefox are on the{" "}
            <a href="https://github.com/GrantTotinov/PlainSight/releases/latest"
              >GitHub releases page</a
            >. In Chrome or Edge, unzip the build, turn on developer mode on the
            extensions page and load the folder as an unpacked extension.
          </li>
        </ul>
      </div>
    </section>

    <section id="pixelforge" class="card mt-6 p-5 sm:p-8">
      <ProjectCard project={PROJECTS.pixelForge} headingLevel="h2" />

      <div class={prose}>
        <h3>What it does</h3>
        <ul>
          <li>
            Reads PNG, JPEG, WebP, GIF (first frame), BMP, ICO and WBMP, and
            writes JPEG, PNG and WebP, lossy or lossless.
          </li>
          <li>
            Fixes orientation from EXIF, resizes with crop, pad, max, min and
            stretch modes, and crops, rotates, flips and converts to grayscale.
          </li>
          <li>
            Uses the same <code>Load</code>, <code>Mutate</code> and
            <code>Save</code> calls as ImageSharp. For most upload and thumbnail code,
            moving over means changing the <code>using</code> lines and the encoder
            calls. The{" "}
            <a
              href="https://github.com/GrantTotinov/PixelForge/blob/main/docs/migrating-from-imagesharp.md"
              >migration guide</a
            > has the full mapping.
          </li>
        </ul>

        <h3>How it's built</h3>
        <ul>
          <li>
            Built on SkiaSharp and licensed under MIT. There's no license key,
            registration or build-time check, so it works the same in open
            source, closed source and CI.
          </li>
          <li>
            Safe by default for user uploads. Decompression bombs are rejected
            from the file header before any pixel memory is allocated, input
            size is capped, and EXIF metadata, including GPS location, is never
            written to the output.
          </li>
          <li>
            JPEG and WebP can be decoded straight at a smaller size, which makes
            thumbnails fast. A 200 px thumbnail from a 12 MP JPEG takes 32.6 ms,
            the fastest of PixelForge, ImageSharp, NetVips and Magick.NET in my
            benchmarks. Full-size resizes are slower than ImageSharp and
            NetVips. The{" "}
            <a href="https://github.com/GrantTotinov/PixelForge#benchmarks"
              >README</a
            > has all the numbers.
          </li>
          <li>
            Runs on Windows, macOS, and Debian, Ubuntu and Alpine containers, on
            x64 and arm64, without installing extra system packages. CI tests
            every one of these.
          </li>
        </ul>

        <h3>Installing it</h3>
        <ul>
          <li>
            From{" "}
            <a href="https://www.nuget.org/packages/PixelForge">NuGet</a>:
            <code>dotnet add package PixelForge</code>.
          </li>
          <li>Needs .NET 8 or newer.</li>
        </ul>
      </div>
    </section>

    <section id="uploadguard" class="card mt-6 p-5 sm:p-8">
      <ProjectCard project={PROJECTS.uploadGuard} headingLevel="h2" />

      <div class={prose}>
        <h3>What it does</h3>
        <ul>
          <li>
            Detects 47 file types from their bytes alone, then checks that the
            extension and the Content-Type belong to the type it found.
          </li>
          <li>
            Named allow-list policies with size and archive limits, checked when
            the app starts. A Minimal API filter and an MVC attribute reject bad
            files with a 400 validation problem before your handler runs.
          </li>
          <li>
            Looks inside the file: zip bombs and zip slip, macros in Office and
            OpenDocument files, JavaScript and launch actions in PDFs (also
            inside compressed object streams), scripts in SVG, and HTML or PHP
            hidden in images.
          </li>
          <li>
            Optional ClamAV scanning with UploadGuard.ClamAV, and image
            re-encoding with PixelForge in UploadGuard.PixelForge, which keeps
            the pixels and drops everything else.
          </li>
        </ul>

        <h3>How it's built</h3>
        <ul>
          <li>
            Files are read in 64 KB chunks and never loaded whole. ZIP limits
            come from the central directory, so nothing is unpacked to check
            them.
          </li>
          <li>
            It fails closed: when a scanner errors or times out, the upload is
            rejected.
          </li>
          <li>
            Accepted files get random 128-bit names, and the app refuses to
            start if the storage folder is inside the web root.
          </li>
          <li>
            Of 14 test files, from a normal JPEG to a ZIP bomb, it handles all
            14 correctly. Mime-Detective and File.TypeChecker, which match
            signatures, get 4 and 6 of them right. The{" "}
            <a href="/posts/uploadguard-secure-file-uploads/">write-up</a> has the
            comparison and how each check works.
          </li>
          <li>
            Licensed under MIT, with no dependencies beyond ASP.NET Core in the
            core package.
          </li>
        </ul>

        <h3>Installing it</h3>
        <ul>
          <li>
            From{" "}
            <a href="https://www.nuget.org/packages/UploadGuard">NuGet</a>:
            <code>dotnet add package UploadGuard --prerelease</code>. The ClamAV
            and PixelForge integrations are separate packages,
            UploadGuard.ClamAV and UploadGuard.PixelForge.
          </li>
          <li>Needs .NET 10 or newer.</li>
        </ul>
      </div>
    </section>
  </Main>

  <Footer />
</Layout>
